[Music]  I'm generally quite suspicious of any  claims to universalism generally because  they all seem to emanate from within the  West and tend to historically have been  tired to expansionist projects  we don't want equality with unequal men  and we can't have equality in an unequal  world a world where wealth flows  upstream a world of gross and growing  inequality that has brought us to the  brink of a planet crisis  feminism as a term is so misunderstood  that rethinking feminism is also finding  a new language in which we can anchor  the change that we want to see  when you travel in Turkey all across the  Middle East you will notice that streets  belong to men public squares belong to  men particularly after certain hours  cities belong to men so there is a  backlash  well welcome everybody and thank you  very much to coming to what promises to  be I think a really interesting debate I  know I'm looking forward to it so I hope  that you are too  this debate is part of a series in  association with Unilever which is  trying to explore feminism and gender  from cross cultural perspectives we've  had a lot of debates on feminism in  Britain and in the u.s. in the West and  I think it's actually very interesting  to try to make it a bit more  international Unilever have very kindly  sponsored not just this debate but also  three more at the how the light gets in  festival in May and first of all I'm  going to introduce all our panelists and  then I'll tell you what the debate is  going to be about first of all we have  Miriam Francois on my far-right stage  left who's a political commentator  theorist and broadcaster with a focus on  France I know French female political  journalists and my sort of job rose up  as one quite recently to complain about  her sexist the politicians were so I'm  sure you've had a lot to do that she  writes for the New Statesman The  Guardian and The Huffington Post and is  also doing a DPhil in Middle Eastern  Studies at Oxford where she's  researching Islamic political movements  in Morocco Elif Shafak  is an award-winning novelist from Turkey  she's been published in more than 40  countries she's been awarded as  Chevalier of the order of Arts and  Letters and she's also been an outspoken  defender of women's rights minority  rights and freedom of expression fin  Makai is an activist on academic  described by the Guardian as a  world-changing woman for her political  work defending women's rights she's it's  nothing  [Laughter]  she's the founder of the London feminist  Network and a regular public commentator  on feminist issues particularly male  violence towards women and is also  senior lecturer in sociology at UE  University of the West of England in  Bristol and Lina Nair is chief human  resources officer at Unilever which is  actually a hell of achievement because  she is the first woman ever to hold that  post in Unilever's several hundred year  history and is on the executive board  and before that she had a wide range of  management jobs in India and South Asia  and spearheaded the career by choice  program which was helping women rejoin  the workforce after a career break so a  pretty diverse panel and we're going to  be we we're going to be talking about  where the feminism fights for equality  for everyone who's doing the fighting  who are they defending and is it right  for women in the West to fight for  equality for women in the developing  world or should they be fighting for it  for themselves from the right to wear  head scarfs to the refusal of  contraception ideas of what is fair and  what is equal very dramatically across  the world so does feminism like a  universal girl and if it does is that  because it's an impossible dream and is  there anything wrong with white Western  liberal feminists which I suppose I'll  describe myself as fighting for the  rights of women from different cultures  should we recognize that feminism means  different things in different places or  should we all be fighting for the same  thing so the format is going to be that  each of our speakers is going to do a  three-minute pitch on should feminism  pursue a universal goal and can there be  one and then we're going to start  discussing amongst ourselves so first of  all Marion  well just in three minutes a few points  that I wanted to make I'm generally  quite suspicious of any claims to  universalism generally because they all  seem to emanate from within the West and  tend to historically have been tired to  expansionist projects and more recently  to various forms of imperialism and as  such I think that they've the discourse  on universalism has functioned as a  supporting ideology of imperialism and  continues to do so in many ways and  certainly feminism has been no exception  we saw for example the co-optation of  feminist ideals when it came to  Afghanistan when it's come to Iraq but  we see it more locally as well I've  worked on more uk-based stories related  to Muslim women and the extent to which  predominant liberal views concerning  religion take precedence over the actual  values and ideals that help women from  minority communities find their own  version of what they would consider to  be emancipation or balance and therefore  I think to a large extent what parades  as universalism masquerades as a form of  cultural imperialism and ultimately  functions as a means of perpetuating  cultural hierarchies the notion that we  in the west of purveyors of a superior  way of looking at the world and that we  need to help others benefit from that  and if they don't want to help them will  militarily assist them in seeing what's  best for them I'm also suspicious of  discourses about universes and within  feminism without our broader discussion  about inequality more broadly so to  paraphrase bell hooks how do we talk  about men and women being equal when all  men with a capital m on  call clearly there are social and  economic hierarchies so governing our  global system and it seems a little  bizarre to me to talk about equality  between women in Dhaka and women in  London when the economic differences  between people in London and people in  Dhaka are so extreme and finally I don't  agree with the gender-blind model which  I think has become quite dominant in the  feminist discourse I think what again  gets discussed in terms of gender  blindness is often the idea that we as  women have to imitate male standards in  order to achieve equality and actually  to my mind I don't know that men have  been doing that good a job so maybe we  don't want to replicate what they've  been doing maybe we need to find our own  way of doing things and maybe they've  got something to learn from that rather  than just trying to imitate what men  have been doing and considering that to  be our version of equality I think  that's chasing false dreams and I think  it's chasing also a notion of  emancipation which is tied into our  capitalist system which considers the  only value of a human being to be based  on their productive capacity so your  ability to produce X amount of money is  what your value as a human is worth and  maybe we as women who actually you know  produce real-life human people and I  think much more valuable things than  just money could could offer alternative  views on what is valuable what we should  consider to be yea valuable within  society more broadly  well I'd like to start by saying there's  probably as many different definitions  of feminism as there are people who call  themselves feminists for me  feminism is a global political movement  for the liberation of women and society  based on equality for all people now  however we define it what is agreed is  that this movement is in resurgence  today a resurgence that began unfolding  in the early 2000s here in this country  and indeed around the world sometimes  it's called a third or even a fourth  wave feminist activism is visible once  again online and on the streets  commentary and feminist political theory  is also being seen in the mainstream in  a way that it just wasn't before  you

